title: Anatomy of the Axilla
---

# Anatomy of the Axilla

- **Axilla** - body region serving as transition between neck and arm:
  - **Axillary inlet** - triangular structure with apex at the coracoid process:
    - <u>Base of the axillary inlet</u> - lateral border of 1st rib
    - <u>Anterior margin of axillary inlet</u> - posterior border of clavicle
    - <u>Posterior margin of axillary inlet</u> - superior border of the scapulae
  - **Borders of the axilla:**
    - <u>Anterior axillary wall</u> - P. major, P. minor (deep in clavicopectorial fascia), subclavius
    - <u>Medial axillary wall</u> - serratus anterior (innervated by long thoracic nerve), and associated tissue of chest wall
    - <u>Lateral axillary wall</u> - intertubercular groove of the humerus
    - <u>Posterior axillary wall</u> - subscapularis, distal parts of latissimus dorsi and teres major, long head of triceps brachi
  - **Structures within the axilla:**
    - <u>Muscles</u> - bicep brachii, corocobrachialis
    - <u>Arteries</u> - axillary artery (extension of the subclavian a.)
    - <u>Veins</u> - axillary vein
    - <u>Nerves</u> - Trunks of brachial plexus and associated branches, including long thoracic nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, and intercostobrachial nerve
    - <u>Lymphatics</u> - axillary LN (level I-III)
- **Axillary lymph nodes:**
  - **Grouped into 3 anatomical levels:**
    - <u>Level I axillary LN</u> - lateral to lateral border of P. minor
    - <u>Level II axillary LN</u> - posterior to P. minor and in between the two pectoralis muscles (Rotter's or interpectorial nodes)
    - <u>Level III axillary LN</u> - medial to medial border of P. minor and includes the supraclavicular LN
  - **Physiology of lymphatic drainage in the axilla:**
- **Important nerves in the axilla** - branches from the trunks and cords of the brachial plexus:
  - <u>Long thoracic nerve</u> - motor innervation to the serratus anterior muscle, important for fixation of scapula to chest wall and shoulder adduction and extension (related to **Winging of Scapulae Deformity**)
  - <u>Thoracodorsal nerve</u> (branch of posterior cord) - motor innervation to to Latissimus dorsi
  - <u>Intercostobrachial nerve</u> - sensory innervation to medial aspect of the arm
  - <u>Medial pectorial nerve</u> (branch of medial cord) - motor innervation to P. major
